--- A bare `return` in a function that answers nothing but can fail does not build compiled.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i): prints
---     big
---     small
--- Compiled (crow run): the program does not build:
---     error[E0069]: `return;` in a function whose return type is not `()`
---       --> src/main.rs:33:9
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what Crow
---     accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: the same output as interpreted.
---
--- The condition decides on a value that can fail (`int[s]`), so `check` is typed as one
--- that can fail and the emitter writes `fn check(s: &String) -> CrowResult<()>`; the bare
--- `return` is printed as `return;`, where the end of the body is wrapped as
--- `return CrowResult { ok: true, value: (), … }`. The same with `text = input[]` and
--- `if text eq 'q'`. Without the fallible condition (an int parameter) it builds.
--- Found writing rosetta/15_puzzle_game.co (a quit branch in the game's loop).

fn check[s]
    n = int[s]
    if n gt 5
        log['big']
        return
    log['small']

check['7']
check['2']
